{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"b5f87ff078a8cfea","name":"card_event_surprise_engagement","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/USA/OceaniaStorm/events/card_event_surprise_engagement","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"SURPRISE ENGAGEMENT","text":"Costs 2 less if Rain was played this turn. Destroy a random enemy unit. Draw the top unit of your deck.","flavorText":"The Battle of the Driniumor River was one of the most brutal surprise infantry assaults of the New Guinea campaign. In 1944, US forces near Aitape were pushing westward while also guarding airfields and supply lines. Across the river stood elements of the Japanese 18th Army, already weakened by hunger and disease but still capable of a dangerous attack. In July 1944, Japanese infantry crossed the Driniumor at night and struck the forward positions of the US 32nd Infantry Division. The attack achieved surprise in several sectors. Outposts were overrun, communications broke down, and small groups of American infantry had to fight in isolation in thick jungle. For a time, the fighting became a confused struggle of patrol clashes, ambushes, and desperate local defenses. The Americans recovered by using artillery, mortars, and air support to break up Japanese attacks, then launched counterattacks to restore the line. As the battle continued, the Japanese offensive lost strength. The fighting around the Driniumor river showed how quickly a quiet jungle front could erupt into a brutal infantry battle at close range.","faction":"USA","type":"order","rarity":"Rare","cost":5,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_surprise_engagement","title":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_surprise_engagement_title","SourceString":"SURPRISE ENGAGEMENT","LocalizedString":"SURPRISE ENGAGEMENT"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::USA","kredits":5,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_surprise_engagement_text","SourceString":"Costs 2 less if Rain was played this turn. Destroy a random enemy unit. Draw the top unit of your deck.","LocalizedString":"Costs 2 less if Rain was played this turn. Destroy a random enemy unit. Draw the top unit of your deck."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_surprise_engagement_history","SourceString":"The Battle of the Driniumor River was one of the most brutal surprise infantry assaults of the New Guinea campaign. In 1944, US forces near Aitape were pushing westward while also guarding airfields and supply lines. Across the river stood elements of the Japanese 18th Army, already weakened by hunger and disease but still capable of a dangerous attack. In July 1944, Japanese infantry crossed the Driniumor at night and struck the forward positions of the US 32nd Infantry Division. The attack achieved surprise in several sectors. Outposts were overrun, communications broke down, and small groups of American infantry had to fight in isolation in thick jungle. For a time, the fighting became a confused struggle of patrol clashes, ambushes, and desperate local defenses. The Americans recovered by using artillery, mortars, and air support to break up Japanese attacks, then launched counterattacks to restore the line. As the battle continued, the Japanese offensive lost strength. The fighting around the Driniumor river showed how quickly a quiet jungle front could erupt into a brutal infantry battle at close range.","LocalizedString":"The Battle of the Driniumor River was one of the most brutal surprise infantry assaults of the New Guinea campaign. In 1944, US forces near Aitape were pushing westward while also guarding airfields and supply lines. Across the river stood elements of the Japanese 18th Army, already weakened by hunger and disease but still capable of a dangerous attack. In July 1944, Japanese infantry crossed the Driniumor at night and struck the forward positions of the US 32nd Infantry Division. The attack achieved surprise in several sectors. Outposts were overrun, communications broke down, and small groups of American infantry had to fight in isolation in thick jungle. For a time, the fighting became a confused struggle of patrol clashes, ambushes, and desperate local defenses. The Americans recovered by using artillery, mortars, and air support to break up Japanese attacks, then launched counterattacks to restore the line. As the battle continued, the Japanese offensive lost strength. The fighting around the Driniumor river showed how quickly a quiet jungle front could erupt into a brutal infantry battle at close range."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Rare","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::OceaniaStorm","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardPlayedFromHand","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnEndOfTurn"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3.0"},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/SURPRISE_ENGAGEMENT.SURPRISE_ENGAGEMENT","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_surprise_engagement_photo","SourceString":"US infantry landing on the shores of Aitape, New Guinea, 1944.","LocalizedString":"US infantry landing on the shores of Aitape, New Guinea, 1944."},"OnPlayedFromHandVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_OrderDiveBomber.BP_OrderDiveBomber_C","SubPathString":""},"isRemoval":true},"variables":[{"name":"rainFound","type":"bool","direction":"in"},{"name":"cardFound","type":"bool","direction":"in"},{"name":"unitOnTop","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_event_surprise_engagement","owner":"card_event_surprise_engagement_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_Final | FUNC_UbergraphFunction | FUNC_HasDefaults","locals":[{"name":"Temp_bool_True_if_